Core gives a department the ability to define a regime as a hierarchy entirely of its own choosing: optional schedules, each made up of one or more sections, each made up of a set of questions — with the route through any section's questions free to depend on the answers given along the way. That structure and content is departmental knowledge for every regime, without exception; core supplies the engine that runs it and the tooling to author it, nothing more.

Once that structure exists, a department brings it to life for citizens through a single mechanism, used identically every time: an action — a call to core naming exactly which sections or schedules to work through this time, call_core(these items) — paired with two things the department controls and nothing else: what it does immediately before making that call, and what it does the moment core hands the citizen back.

For the simplest regimes, one action is enough: a single "Apply" button on the regime home page, whose before-call step is trivial and whose call is call_core(everything), and whose on-return step is simply nothing further — the citizen is home, done. This isn't a cut-down use of the mechanism; it's the same mechanism with the least possible either side of it.

For more complex regimes, a department defines several actions instead of one — each its own call_core(...) naming a smaller, specific piece of the regime, each with its own before-and-after code. Structurally nothing changes between one action and many: same call, same shape, same two hooks. What changes is only how much a department chooses to put into those hooks. On return from one action, a department is free to do whatever the regime actually requires: decide which action should appear next, given what's just been answered — an early knockout question determines which later sections are even relevant, so an action for property only appears once the citizen's own answers have justified it — or do something with the answers that has nothing to do with sequencing at all, such as checking whether this deceased's estate already exists in the system before letting an IHT executor proceed, and establishing the identity the rest of that estate's answers will belong to. Both belong at exactly the same point, because both are simply the department doing what its regime needs done with what it now knows, before deciding what the citizen sees next.

So the whole range, from the simplest regime to IHT, is one idea rather than two: a set of actions, each a single call_core naming this time's work, each with its own entry and exit. A department defines more actions, and puts more into their entry and exit, exactly to the degree its own regime calls for it — never a second pattern to learn, only the same one used once, or used as many times as the regime needs.